1
00:00:05,010 --> 00:00:09,010
Welcome to the 6th lecture of this MOOC, Introduction to Virus Management.

2
00:00:09,010 --> 00:00:15,010
In this lecture, I will be discussing the World Health Organization Laboratory Biosafety Manual

3
00:00:15,010 --> 00:00:18,010
and the Pathogen Safety Data Sheets.

4
00:00:18,010 --> 00:00:24,010
These are essential guidelines in order to assess the risk posed by known biological agents.

5
00:00:24,010 --> 00:00:31,010
Please note that all risk assessment must be done using known biological agents as a reference.

6
00:00:31,010 --> 00:00:35,010
We cannot conduct a risk assessment for unknown biological agents.

7
00:00:35,010 --> 00:00:47,270
Welcome back to this lecture on risk assessment.

8
00:00:47,270 --> 00:00:51,270
In today's lecture, I will discuss the Pathogen Safety with Data Sheets

9
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:57,270
as well as specific guidelines in the World Health Organization Laboratory Biosafety Manual

10
00:00:57,270 --> 00:01:00,270
pertaining to risk assessment.

11
00:01:00,270 --> 00:01:07,620
The learning objectives for this lecture are as follows.

12
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:13,620
I will introduce you to the process of risk assessment for a known biological agent.

13
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:19,620
Please take note that risk assessment can only be conducted for known biological agents.

14
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:23,620
We cannot conduct a risk assessment for unknown biological agents

15
00:01:23,620 --> 00:01:28,620
as the risk is not perceptible or the risk is unknown.

16
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:32,620
We will discuss the key points that must be addressed during risk assessment

17
00:01:32,620 --> 00:01:35,620
pertaining to the characteristics of the pathogen.

18
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:39,620
And we will also discuss the Pathogen Safety Data Sheets,

19
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:47,620
which is a very important document that is utilized currently by risk managers across the world.

20
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:53,620
And we have to be thankful to the Government of Canada for developing this excellent resource.

21
00:01:53,620 --> 00:01:58,830
The learning outcomes for this module are as follows.

22
00:01:58,830 --> 00:02:05,830
Upon completion of this module, I want you to demonstrate the ability to develop a questionnaire

23
00:02:05,830 --> 00:02:11,830
or an instrument for risk assessment, address specific criteria pertaining to risk assessment

24
00:02:11,830 --> 00:02:17,830
based on your own localized situation, and apply the information from the PSDS

25
00:02:17,830 --> 00:02:21,830
to assess the risk posed by a known biological agent.

26
00:02:21,830 --> 00:02:28,740
Risk assessment functions within a scope.

27
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:32,740
There are risks that are within the scope of an assessment

28
00:02:32,740 --> 00:02:35,740
and risks that are beyond the scope of an assessment.

29
00:02:35,740 --> 00:02:39,740
This is the iceberg analogy in which this is the risk that you see

30
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:44,740
and which you perceive, and this is the risk which you do not see and cannot perceive.

31
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:53,500
The World Health Organization Laboratory Biosafety Manual

32
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:58,500
contains specific guidelines for microbial logical risk assessment.

33
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:04,810
These focus on specific criteria or characteristics of the microbe,

34
00:03:04,810 --> 00:03:07,810
such as the pathogenicity of the microbe,

35
00:03:07,810 --> 00:03:11,810
the potential outcome of exposure in terms of morbidity and mortality,

36
00:03:11,810 --> 00:03:16,810
the natural route of infection, other routes of infection,

37
00:03:16,810 --> 00:03:20,810
stability of the biological agent in the environment,

38
00:03:20,810 --> 00:03:24,810
concentration and culture volume of the biological agent

39
00:03:24,810 --> 00:03:29,810
with respect to the specific laboratory procedure or laboratory operation,

40
00:03:29,810 --> 00:03:34,810
as well as the presence of a suitable host or vector

41
00:03:34,810 --> 00:03:36,810
for transmission of the biological agent,

42
00:03:36,810 --> 00:03:40,810
the priori information available from researchers

43
00:03:40,810 --> 00:03:45,810
and other studies pertaining to that particular biological agent,

44
00:03:45,810 --> 00:03:49,810
specific laboratory procedures which increase the risk of exposure

45
00:03:49,810 --> 00:03:54,810
such as pipetting, centrifugation and extraction of nucleic acids,

46
00:03:54,810 --> 00:03:57,810
any genetic manipulation of the organism

47
00:03:57,810 --> 00:04:00,810
in case of genetically modified organism

48
00:04:00,810 --> 00:04:05,810
which has been incorporated or endowed with specific traits

49
00:04:05,810 --> 00:04:09,810
such as the increase in host range or the transmission,

50
00:04:09,810 --> 00:04:14,810
local availability of effective prophylaxis or therapeutic interventions

51
00:04:14,810 --> 00:04:17,810
with respect to that particular biological agent.

52
00:04:17,810 --> 00:04:21,810
Please take note that each biological risk assessment

53
00:04:21,810 --> 00:04:26,810
must be conducted for a specific biological agent.

54
00:04:26,810 --> 00:04:30,810
For instance, if you have developed a risk assessment

55
00:04:30,810 --> 00:04:33,810
for mycobacterium tuberculosis,

56
00:04:33,810 --> 00:04:36,810
the same risk assessment cannot be utilized for pseudomonas,

57
00:04:36,810 --> 00:04:39,810
agenosa or coronavirus.

58
00:04:39,810 --> 00:04:42,810
The risk assessment must also take into account

59
00:04:42,810 --> 00:04:45,810
the current availability of facilities

60
00:04:45,810 --> 00:04:51,340
as well as the training elements.

61
00:04:51,340 --> 00:04:59,580
This is a specific questionnaire or an instrument for risk assessment.

62
00:04:59,580 --> 00:05:02,580
Now, the questions have to be very specific

63
00:05:02,580 --> 00:05:05,580
and the answer will be either yes or no.

64
00:05:05,580 --> 00:05:08,580
This is an example which you can adopt or adapt

65
00:05:08,580 --> 00:05:11,580
to your own respective facilities.

66
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:14,580
The first question is, has the biological agent

67
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:17,580
been reported to be a pathogen?

68
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:23,930
In this case, prior information is required, a prior report.

69
00:05:23,930 --> 00:05:26,930
The next question focuses on morbidity.

70
00:05:26,930 --> 00:05:30,930
Does exposure to the biological agent lead to morbidity?

71
00:05:30,930 --> 00:05:33,930
So you have yes or no, as the case may be.

72
00:05:33,930 --> 00:05:37,930
The route of infection, is it via inhalation?

73
00:05:37,930 --> 00:05:43,930
So this may then lead to your risk mitigation strategy,

74
00:05:43,930 --> 00:05:46,930
which involves the usage of respiratory protection

75
00:05:46,930 --> 00:05:52,360
for inhalation such as masks.

76
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:55,360
Route of infection via contact, yes or no?

77
00:05:55,360 --> 00:05:57,360
Stability in the environment,

78
00:05:57,360 --> 00:06:00,360
is the biological agent viable outside of the host

79
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:03,360
or does it basically get degraded in the environment,

80
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:06,360
as in the case of most viruses?

81
00:06:06,360 --> 00:06:08,360
Genetic modifications which may have been carried out

82
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:12,360
by the researchers and the availability of treatment.

83
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:15,360
These are the basic questions which you must address

84
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:17,360
as a virus manager.

85
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:21,500
We then move on to some of the questions

86
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:25,500
which you must pose to your laboratory workers

87
00:06:25,500 --> 00:06:27,500
or your principal investigators.

88
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:29,500
The first question pertains to training.

89
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:32,500
Has adequate training been provided to the laboratory workers?

90
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:35,500
If not, additional training must be provided.

91
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:37,500
So it's either yes or no.

92
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:39,500
Contingency plans.

93
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:42,500
These must be put into place in the event of a spill

94
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:45,500
or in the event of a breach of containment.

95
00:06:45,500 --> 00:06:47,500
The SOPs must be developed.

96
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:50,500
And if there's a yes, you click on yes.

97
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:53,500
If there's a no, you just select no.

98
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:54,500
Facility.

99
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:57,500
And the facility pertains to the essential equipment,

100
00:06:57,500 --> 00:06:59,500
which is based on the risk group.

101
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:01,500
One of the essential equipments,

102
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:04,500
which I've discussed earlier, is the biological safety cabinet.

103
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:08,500
The availability of the personal protective equipment.

104
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:11,500
The availability of the waste disposal system

105
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:14,500
to prevent release of the biological agent

106
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:18,500
into the environment or breach of the secondary containment.

107
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:20,500
The security of the facility,

108
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:24,500
as well as specific SOPs related to managing the pathogen.

109
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:26,500
As you can see,

110
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:29,500
most of these questions pertain to processes

111
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:34,500
within the facility itself and the specific controls.

112
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:37,500
And one must conduct this kind of a risk assessment

113
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:40,500
prior to moving on to risk mitigation.

114
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:47,760
We now move on to the pathogen safety data sheets.

115
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:49,760
I will be discussing the template itself

116
00:07:49,760 --> 00:07:54,760
and the various criteria which are described in the templates.

117
00:07:54,760 --> 00:07:59,550
This is the pathogen safety data sheet website.

118
00:07:59,550 --> 00:08:02,550
It is a current document which is updated

119
00:08:02,550 --> 00:08:04,550
as information becomes available.

120
00:08:04,550 --> 00:08:07,550
And as I mentioned earlier,

121
00:08:07,550 --> 00:08:09,550
I would like to thank the Government of Canada

122
00:08:09,550 --> 00:08:12,550
for developing this excellent resource material,

123
00:08:12,550 --> 00:08:16,550
which is a lifesaver for many biorisk managers.

124
00:08:16,550 --> 00:08:20,250
This is the Android version,

125
00:08:20,250 --> 00:08:22,250
which is available in the form of an app.

126
00:08:22,250 --> 00:08:25,250
You can also find it for other mobile phones,

127
00:08:25,250 --> 00:08:34,810
such as the Apple.

128
00:08:34,810 --> 00:08:36,810
There are nine sections,

129
00:08:36,810 --> 00:08:38,810
which are described in the template.

130
00:08:38,810 --> 00:08:41,810
And if you key in the biological agent

131
00:08:41,810 --> 00:08:43,810
into the search engine

132
00:08:43,810 --> 00:08:46,810
on this particular pathogen safety data sheet website,

133
00:08:46,810 --> 00:08:49,810
it will then direct you to these sections.

134
00:08:49,810 --> 00:08:53,810
And the section describes the infectious agent

135
00:08:53,810 --> 00:08:56,810
as well as the characteristics of the infectious agent.

136
00:08:56,810 --> 00:09:00,810
I will go through the sections one at a time.

137
00:09:00,810 --> 00:09:04,810
And this will clarify any questions pertaining to the sections

138
00:09:04,810 --> 00:09:07,810
and their respective content.

139
00:09:07,810 --> 00:09:10,810
So section one focuses on the infectious agent.

140
00:09:10,810 --> 00:09:13,810
The scientific name, the agent type,

141
00:09:13,810 --> 00:09:15,810
whether it's a virus, bacterium, fungus,

142
00:09:15,810 --> 00:09:17,810
or prion is indicated.

143
00:09:17,810 --> 00:09:20,810
The taxonomy is indicated in terms of its nomenclature

144
00:09:20,810 --> 00:09:21,810
and classification.

145
00:09:21,810 --> 00:09:24,810
The synonyms, common names,

146
00:09:24,810 --> 00:09:26,810
the characteristics of the biological agent

147
00:09:26,810 --> 00:09:28,810
in terms of its culture characteristics

148
00:09:28,810 --> 00:09:31,810
as well as requirement for a specific medium.

149
00:09:31,810 --> 00:09:34,810
The brief description of the biological agent itself

150
00:09:34,810 --> 00:09:36,810
and its life cycle.

151
00:09:36,810 --> 00:09:38,810
The properties of the infectious agent,

152
00:09:38,810 --> 00:09:42,810
which relate to its properties outside of the host.

153
00:09:42,810 --> 00:09:48,400
Section two focuses on hazard identification.

154
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:50,400
In this section,

155
00:09:50,400 --> 00:09:53,400
the pathogenity and toxicity are addressed.

156
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:55,400
Communicability,

157
00:09:55,400 --> 00:09:57,400
the ability of the biological agent

158
00:09:57,400 --> 00:10:00,400
to be transmitted across the community.

159
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:04,400
The epidemiology, the host range.

160
00:10:04,400 --> 00:10:08,400
The infectious dose and the incubation period.

161
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:11,400
The incubation period is the duration

162
00:10:11,400 --> 00:10:15,400
between the exposure to the biological agent

163
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:21,060
and the manifestation of the symptoms.

164
00:10:21,060 --> 00:10:24,060
Section three focuses on dissemination.

165
00:10:24,060 --> 00:10:29,060
It indicates if a reservoir exists.

166
00:10:29,060 --> 00:10:31,060
The zoonosis and river zoonosis

167
00:10:31,060 --> 00:10:35,060
is the biological agent transmitted via an animal

168
00:10:35,060 --> 00:10:39,060
and specific vectors.

169
00:10:39,060 --> 00:10:41,060
So the reservoir for the biological agent

170
00:10:41,060 --> 00:10:46,060
may be a human host or it may also be an animal host

171
00:10:46,060 --> 00:10:52,060
or an insect.

172
00:10:52,060 --> 00:10:54,060
Section four focuses on the stability

173
00:10:54,060 --> 00:10:57,060
and viability of the biological agent.

174
00:10:57,060 --> 00:10:59,060
As you must be well aware by now,

175
00:10:59,060 --> 00:11:02,060
there are multiple cases of drug resistance

176
00:11:02,060 --> 00:11:05,060
or antibiotic resistance in microbes.

177
00:11:05,060 --> 00:11:10,060
And these have been reported across the world.

178
00:11:10,060 --> 00:11:13,060
And this must be addressed in terms of risk assessment

179
00:11:13,060 --> 00:11:15,060
for stability and viability.

180
00:11:15,060 --> 00:11:18,060
The susceptibility to disinfectants is another issue

181
00:11:18,060 --> 00:11:21,060
which must be addressed as disinfectants

182
00:11:21,060 --> 00:11:24,060
are constantly used in the laboratory

183
00:11:24,060 --> 00:11:27,590
in routine cleanup.

184
00:11:27,590 --> 00:11:29,590
The conditions for physical inactivation,

185
00:11:29,590 --> 00:11:32,590
which may include heat and the application of steam

186
00:11:32,590 --> 00:11:34,590
or ultraviolet radiation,

187
00:11:34,590 --> 00:11:36,590
as well as the ability of the microorganism

188
00:11:36,590 --> 00:11:38,590
to survive outside of the host.

189
00:11:38,590 --> 00:11:41,590
All of these pertain to the stability

190
00:11:41,590 --> 00:11:43,590
and viability of the microorganism

191
00:11:43,590 --> 00:11:45,590
or biological agent.

192
00:11:45,590 --> 00:11:51,190
Section five must address the available

193
00:11:51,190 --> 00:11:53,190
first aid and medical measures,

194
00:11:53,190 --> 00:11:56,190
which include surveillance of the laboratory personnel.

195
00:11:56,190 --> 00:12:02,190
This may be in the terms of a baseline serum study.

196
00:12:02,190 --> 00:12:04,190
First aid and treatment in the event

197
00:12:04,190 --> 00:12:06,190
of breach of containment,

198
00:12:06,190 --> 00:12:08,190
vaccination if it exists,

199
00:12:08,190 --> 00:12:10,190
and profile access of preventive treatment

200
00:12:10,190 --> 00:12:13,190
for the laboratory workers.

201
00:12:13,190 --> 00:12:18,940
Section six focuses on laboratory hazards.

202
00:12:18,940 --> 00:12:21,940
Laboratory hazards are the result of working

203
00:12:21,940 --> 00:12:23,940
with the biological agent.

204
00:12:23,940 --> 00:12:26,940
And these are likely to be LAIs

205
00:12:26,940 --> 00:12:28,940
or laboratory acquired infection

206
00:12:28,940 --> 00:12:30,940
or hospital acquired infections,

207
00:12:30,940 --> 00:12:31,940
as the case may be.

208
00:12:31,940 --> 00:12:33,940
The sources and specimen,

209
00:12:33,940 --> 00:12:35,940
for instance, if a laboratory worker

210
00:12:35,940 --> 00:12:38,940
is working with a tissue sample

211
00:12:38,940 --> 00:12:40,940
or a body fluid derived from a patient

212
00:12:40,940 --> 00:12:42,940
with a known biological agent,

213
00:12:42,940 --> 00:12:46,940
the body fluid may also contain unknown biological agents,

214
00:12:46,940 --> 00:12:49,940
which pose a risk to the laboratory worker.

215
00:12:49,940 --> 00:12:51,940
There are primary hazards associated

216
00:12:51,940 --> 00:12:54,940
with the biological agent itself,

217
00:12:54,940 --> 00:12:56,940
as well as specific hazards which are associated

218
00:12:56,940 --> 00:13:00,940
with operations involving the biological agent.

219
00:13:00,940 --> 00:13:05,440
Section seven focuses on exposure control

220
00:13:05,440 --> 00:13:07,440
and personal protection.

221
00:13:07,440 --> 00:13:09,440
In this case,

222
00:13:09,440 --> 00:13:14,330
we must identify the risk group,

223
00:13:14,330 --> 00:13:16,330
risk group one to four,

224
00:13:16,330 --> 00:13:19,330
the level of containment which is currently available

225
00:13:19,330 --> 00:13:21,330
or which is required,

226
00:13:21,330 --> 00:13:23,330
the PPEs of protective clothing

227
00:13:23,330 --> 00:13:25,330
and other protection which may be required

228
00:13:25,330 --> 00:13:28,330
in the case of a biological agent.

229
00:13:28,330 --> 00:13:35,130
Section eight focuses on handling

230
00:13:35,130 --> 00:13:37,130
and storage of the pathogen,

231
00:13:37,130 --> 00:13:40,130
and this encompasses spills in the laboratory,

232
00:13:40,130 --> 00:13:44,130
which may pose a risk to laboratory personnel.

233
00:13:44,130 --> 00:13:46,130
Appropriate SOPs must be available

234
00:13:46,130 --> 00:13:53,020
to address spills, disposal of the biological agent

235
00:13:53,020 --> 00:13:57,020
after completion of the laboratory processes,

236
00:13:57,020 --> 00:14:02,710
and storage of the biological agent in the laboratory

237
00:14:02,710 --> 00:14:04,710
under the ambient conditions

238
00:14:04,710 --> 00:14:06,710
or the ideal conditions for storage

239
00:14:06,710 --> 00:14:12,690
of that particular biological agent.

240
00:14:12,690 --> 00:14:14,690
Section nine focuses on regulatory

241
00:14:14,690 --> 00:14:15,690
and other information.

242
00:14:15,690 --> 00:14:18,690
This may pertain to your specific national

243
00:14:18,690 --> 00:14:20,690
or local regulatory information,

244
00:14:20,690 --> 00:14:25,690
which may be incorporated into your own specific PSDS.

245
00:14:25,690 --> 00:14:28,690
You must also indicate the update

246
00:14:28,690 --> 00:14:32,690
in the event that you do apply this template

247
00:14:32,690 --> 00:14:35,690
within your local conditions,

248
00:14:35,690 --> 00:14:37,690
the name of the content creator

249
00:14:37,690 --> 00:14:39,690
as well as current references

250
00:14:39,690 --> 00:14:43,690
for the development of the PSDS.

251
00:14:43,690 --> 00:14:46,690
What I have gone through is a template layout

252
00:14:47,690 --> 00:14:51,690
for the PSDS, and if you go through the website,

253
00:14:51,690 --> 00:14:56,690
you will find that almost all of the biological agents

254
00:14:56,690 --> 00:14:59,690
have been clearly organized,

255
00:14:59,690 --> 00:15:01,690
and each of their characteristics

256
00:15:01,690 --> 00:15:04,690
has been delineated in the sections.

257
00:15:04,690 --> 00:15:12,130
That brings us to the conclusion

258
00:15:12,130 --> 00:15:15,130
of this lecture on risk assessment.

259
00:15:15,130 --> 00:15:16,130
In this lecture,

260
00:15:16,130 --> 00:15:19,130
we are focused on microbiological risk assessment

261
00:15:19,130 --> 00:15:22,130
based on the WHO LBM.

262
00:15:22,130 --> 00:15:24,130
We have understood the scope of risk assessment.

263
00:15:24,130 --> 00:15:27,130
We now know that risk assessment

264
00:15:27,130 --> 00:15:31,130
pertains to only known biological agents.

265
00:15:31,130 --> 00:15:33,130
Unknown biological agents

266
00:15:33,130 --> 00:15:36,130
cannot be subjected to a risk assessment.

267
00:15:36,130 --> 00:15:40,570
We have looked at the pathogen safety data sheets,

268
00:15:40,570 --> 00:15:42,570
and all of this should lead you

269
00:15:42,570 --> 00:15:46,570
to the development of your own specific risk assessment.

270
00:15:46,570 --> 00:15:49,570
As an exercise for your benefit,

271
